  "narrative": "There are no assessments due, so I am using the 12-analysis capacity to request the registered high-power balanced binary screen for the currently untested outcomes outcome_013, outcome_014, outcome_015, and outcome_016. These correspond to H13-H24 and test feature_034, feature_002, and feature_060 against comparator 0. All three predictors have approximately half the dataset in each cell, so discovery intervals should be substantially tighter than the sparse first-screen comparisons. This prioritizes outcomes with no prior direct evidence while maintaining scientific diversity across predictors. All claims are direction -1, so a positive signed estimate means feature=1 has stronger dependency (more negative outcome) than feature=0; a negative signed estimate would indicate weaker dependency or the opposite shift. Anticipated direction remains 0 because predictor labels are opaque. The clinical benchmark is interpreted as a relative difference of about 10% in outcome magnitude; for outcomes with mean magnitude near -0.25 to -0.27, that is roughly a 0.025 absolute dependency-score shift, with extra caution for outcome_014 because its mean is near zero but its spread is large. If any of H13-H24 produces a clinically sized point estimate or an interval that strongly excludes trivial effects, the next stage should prioritize follow-up or validation. If all 12 are small/null, I would close this outcome-predictor block as high-power but uninformative and move to H25-H36 for high-dispersion outcomes 001, 006, 008, and 011.",
  "research_notes": "Analyze allocation: run H13-H24, the balanced feature_034/feature_002/feature_060 main effects on outcomes 013-016. This completes high-power testing for outcomes with no prior direct evidence while preserving H25-H36 for outcomes 001, 006, 008, and 011 in the next cycle if needed. Interpretation for direction -1: positive signed evidence supports stronger dependency in exposed=1. Acceptance requires clinically meaningful effect and uncertainty that makes a trivial null unlikely; validation only if a new effect is large/stable enough."
